1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,202
Today we revise Chapter Four.

2
00:00:02,202 --> 00:00:05,088
Multimedia elements, and digitisation.

3
00:00:05,088 --> 00:00:09,720
How images, audio, and video change from analogue to digital.

4
00:00:10,070 --> 00:00:13,107
Digitisation has two steps.

5
00:00:13,107 --> 00:00:14,908
First, sampling.

6
00:00:14,908 --> 00:00:17,270
Second, quantization.

7
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,068
Sampling takes discrete amplitude samples

8
00:00:20,068 --> 00:00:23,112
from a continuous analogue signal, at a fixed rate.

9
00:00:23,112 --> 00:00:27,172
A higher sampling rate means more faithful sound or picture quality.

10
00:00:27,522 --> 00:00:30,181
Quantization maps continuous amplitudes

11
00:00:30,181 --> 00:00:32,566
onto preset discrete binary levels.

12
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:35,634
Higher bit depth means a finer dynamic range.

13
00:00:35,984 --> 00:00:40,223
A sound card uses an A D C to turn analogue into digital,

14
00:00:40,223 --> 00:00:43,718
and a D A C to turn digital back into analogue.

15
00:00:43,718 --> 00:00:47,288
A digital camera uses a C C D or C M O S sensor.

16
00:00:47,638 --> 00:00:52,472
Remember the core idea: digitisation is only a discrete approximation.

17
00:00:52,472 --> 00:00:54,958
So there is always information loss.

18
00:00:55,308 --> 00:00:58,655
A bitmap is a rectangular grid of colour pixels.

19
00:00:58,655 --> 00:01:01,932
Quality depends on resolution and colour depth.

20
00:01:02,282 --> 00:01:06,159
When you enlarge a bitmap, it goes jagged and looks distorted.

21
00:01:06,159 --> 00:01:11,786
File size in bytes equals width times height times colour depth in bits, divided by eight.

22
00:01:12,136 --> 00:01:13,851
Four common formats.

23
00:01:13,851 --> 00:01:17,108
B M P: uncompressed, very large files.

24
00:01:17,108 --> 00:01:20,709
J P G: lossy compression, good for photos.

25
00:01:20,709 --> 00:01:25,081
P N G: lossless compression, supports transparency.

26
00:01:25,081 --> 00:01:31,768
G I F: two hundred and fifty-six colours, supports animation and transparency.

27
00:01:32,118 --> 00:01:35,325
A vector graphic is defined by maths and paths.

28
00:01:35,325 --> 00:01:39,282
It is resolution-independent; it stays sharp at any scale.

29
00:01:39,282 --> 00:01:43,854
Files are usually small, but vectors are not suited to real photos.

30
00:01:44,204 --> 00:01:48,535
Common formats: S V G, A I, E P S, and W M F.

31
00:01:48,535 --> 00:01:53,348
S V G is supported on the web and in H T M L five.

32
00:01:53,698 --> 00:01:55,842
Uncompressed image size in megabytes:

33
00:01:55,842 --> 00:01:58,507
width times height times colour depth in bits,

34
00:01:58,507 --> 00:02:03,548
divided by eight times one thousand and twenty-four times one thousand and twenty-four.

35
00:02:03,548 --> 00:02:06,850
After compression, size depends on the compression ratio.

36
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,283
Uncompressed audio size in megabytes:

37
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:13,561
channels times bit depth times sampling rate in hertz times time in seconds,

38
00:02:13,561 --> 00:02:18,458
divided by eight times one thousand and twenty-four times one thousand and twenty-four.

39
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:23,693
Compressed audio: bit rate in kilobits per second, times one thousand, times time in seconds,

40
00:02:23,693 --> 00:02:28,872
then divided by eight times one thousand and twenty-four times one thousand and twenty-four.

41
00:02:29,222 --> 00:02:31,318
Uncompressed video size in gigabytes:

42
00:02:31,318 --> 00:02:36,814
frame width times frame height times colour depth in bits times frame rate times time in seconds,

43
00:02:36,814 --> 00:02:40,100
divided by eight times one thousand and twenty-four cubed.

44
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:45,482
Compressed video: bit rate in megabits per second, times ten to the six, times time in seconds,

45
00:02:45,482 --> 00:02:51,374
divided by eight times one thousand and twenty-four times one thousand and twenty-four — unit megabytes.

46
00:02:51,724 --> 00:02:53,361
Remember three points.

47
00:02:53,361 --> 00:02:58,347
Digitisation: sampling plus quantization — always information loss.

48
00:02:58,347 --> 00:03:02,216
Bitmaps go jagged when enlarged; vectors stay sharp.

49
00:03:02,216 --> 00:03:07,276
For image, audio, and video, lock in the file-size formula keywords.

